    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d156">

                <pb facs="0192" n="184" sameAs="frus:frus1939v02/pg184"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.008 North Pacific/311a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Japan</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-03-17T19:00:00-05:00">March 17, 1938—7 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>93. Tour 177, March 17, 7 p.m.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>With reference to your paragraph 2, the language we have proposed does
                        not, in the Department’s view, involve admission on the part of the Japanese
                        authorities or the Japanese Government that Japanese nationals have fished
                        for salmon in the waters in question. <pb facs="0193" n="185" xml:id="pg185"
                        /> The language used in our draft was designed to avert detailed discussion
                        in regard to what may possibly have occurred heretofore. The assurance under
                        reference was included with a view to meeting a strong and insistent demand
                        here that some reference be made to the reported salmon fishing activities
                        of Japanese vessels in the Bristol Bay area during the 1937 fishing season.
                        The Department has deliberately avoided injection of detailed discussion in
                        regard to evidence in order that the working out of a <hi
                            rendition="#italic">modus vivendi for</hi> the future might not be
                        delayed or prejudiced.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>It is the Department’s intention to retain the final two paragraphs of the
                        Japanese redraft and you should be guided accordingly.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
